;;; An hrule whose `:match` reads `(absent (not (paid ?g)))` stops a debug build —
;;; M1f **W85**, register row `W85/hrule-reads-absent-not`, filed by S1f.7.2 (T1f.7.2.2,
;;; 2026-11-11).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two guests, of whom Bob is known not to have paid, `(not (paid Bob))`, and an
;;; hrule that supposes a guest has paid unless it is known they have not: `(absent
;;; (not (paid ?g)))`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The hrule matches Ann alone, so H is `(paid Ann)` and R is two states, ∅ and
;;; {(paid Ann)}: the goal has no row in the first and `?g = Ann` in the second, which
;;; the `:expect` states. `ein oracle` answers the same R.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `55aadd0b`, release: this R, exit 0, and the claim holds. A build with debug
;;; assertions stopped where `ein solve` took H, exit 101: *an hrule reads `not`,
;;; which hypgen's kill cache writes mid-enumeration* (`hrule.rs`'s `debug_assert!`),
;;; on every solve question, `ein oracle` and `ein test`.
;;; One answer from both builds since S1f.7.2 (T1f.7.2.3).

(relation paid Guest :why "{?1} has paid")
(is-a Ann Guest) (is-a Bob Guest)
(not (paid Bob))
(hrule suppose ()
  :match  (and (is-a ?g Guest) (absent (not (paid ?g))))
  :assert (paid ?g)
  :why "suppose {?g} has paid: nothing says they have not")
(query :goal (paid ?g) :expect (or (model) (model (paid Ann))))
